      AcademyChapter(number: '06', title: 'Pakistan’s political and constitutional system', syllabusLink: 'Constitutional development, federalism, political parties, civil-military relations and democratic governance in Pakistan', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Federalism, institutions and democratic consolidation in Pakistan', objectives: 'Connect constitutional design to political practice, provincial autonomy and democratic accountability.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Constitutional framework', '''The 1973 Constitution establishes a federal parliamentary system, provides for a bicameral Parliament and distributes legislative authority between the federation and provinces. The National Assembly represents population, while the Senate provides equal provincial representation. The Council of Common Interests is an important forum for coordination on matters involving both levels of government. The Eighteenth Amendment substantially altered the distribution of responsibilities and strengthened the constitutional position of provinces.

A good answer identifies the relevant constitutional institution and explains how it is intended to work. Avoid treating federalism as merely administrative decentralisation: it is also a constitutional arrangement for sharing authority.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Political parties and civil-military relations', '''Political parties connect citizens to institutions through elections, policy platforms and parliamentary organisation. Their democratic contribution depends on internal procedures, candidate selection, policy development, financial transparency and respect for constitutional competition. Weak party institutionalisation can increase personalisation and reduce continuity in policy.

Civil-military relations concern the constitutional location of coercive power, civilian oversight, national-security decision-making and the professional role of armed forces. Explain the historical pattern of military interventions and the institutional incentives that affect civilian authority. Avoid monocausal explanations: party weakness, security crises, institutional imbalance and elite bargaining have interacted in different periods.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Democratic consolidation and reform', '''Democratic consolidation requires more than holding elections. It depends on acceptance of constitutional rules, peaceful transfers of power, independent institutions, accountable government, rule of law, effective local government and meaningful citizen participation. Economic inequality, weak service delivery, political polarisation and distrust can undermine legitimacy.

Reform proposals should be specific and implementable. Link each recommendation to an institution, a legal or administrative change, an accountability mechanism and a measurable outcome. For example, strengthening parliamentary committees requires research capacity, timely access to information and follow-up on recommendations.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['1973 Constitution', 'Federalism', 'Eighteenth Amendment', 'Council of Common Interests', 'Civilian oversight', 'Political parties', 'Democratic consolidation'], examQuestion: 'Critically evaluate the working of federalism in Pakistan after the Eighteenth Amendment.', practiceTask: 'Draft a 20-mark answer with constitutional provisions, institutional evidence, implementation problems, counterarguments and practical reforms.'),
      ]),
          AcademyChapter(number: '07', title: 'Political participation, representation and public policy', syllabusLink: 'Political parties, elections, representation, pressure groups, public opinion, civil society and the policy process', lessons: [
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Elections, parties and political representation', objectives: 'Explain how electoral systems translate votes into representation and assess the roles of parties and electoral institutions.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Representation and electoral systems', '''Elections are a mechanism for selecting representatives and authorising governments, but their design shapes political outcomes. Single-member plurality systems can produce strong constituency links yet translate votes into seats unevenly. Proportional systems can reflect vote shares more closely but may produce fragmented legislatures and coalition bargaining. No electoral formula guarantees accountable government: districting, campaign finance, party organisation, media access, election administration and dispute resolution also matter. Evaluate a system against representation, governability, inclusion, accountability and simplicity rather than treating one model as universally ideal.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Political parties and internal organisation', '''Parties aggregate interests, recruit candidates, formulate programmes, organise legislatures and connect citizens with the state. Their performance depends on internal democracy, transparent finance, policy research, local organisation and the ability to discipline elected representatives without suppressing legitimate debate. Personalised or patronage-based parties may mobilise voters but struggle to institutionalise policy commitments. Party institutionalisation is visible when rules and programmes survive leadership transitions and when candidate selection is reasonably predictable and accountable.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Representation beyond election day', '''Representation is continuous, not limited to voting. Constituency service, parliamentary committees, public hearings, constituency offices, accessible information and responsive local government help citizens scrutinise representatives. Descriptive representation concerns whether institutions reflect social diversity; substantive representation concerns whether representatives advance relevant interests. These dimensions may overlap but are not identical. Assess representation by asking whose interests are heard, whose remain excluded and what channels exist for remedy.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Electoral accountability', 'Political party', 'Proportional representation', 'Plurality system', 'Descriptive representation', 'Substantive representation'], examQuestion: 'How do electoral systems and political parties shape the quality of democratic representation?', practiceTask: 'Compare two electoral systems using representation, governability, inclusion and accountability as criteria.'),
        AcademyLesson(title: 'Public opinion, civil society and pressure groups', objectives: 'Distinguish public opinion, civil society organisations, interest groups and social movements and assess their democratic functions.', sections: [
          AcademyLessonSection('1. Public opinion and political communication', '''Public opinion refers to patterns of attitudes held by members of a population on public questions. It is not a single, fixed national view: responses vary with question wording, timing, information, identity and lived experience. Polls require representative sampling, transparent methods and careful interpretation of uncertainty. Media coverage can influence which issues receive attention, but agenda-setting is different from directly determining what every citizen believes. A sound analysis separates measured attitudes from claims made by political actors about what the public wants.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('2. Civil society and interest representation', '''Civil society includes voluntary associations, professional bodies, community organisations, advocacy groups, charities and other organised activity outside the state and market. Such groups can provide services, monitor public institutions, represent marginalised communities and create spaces for civic participation. Their legitimacy depends on accountability, transparent funding, representativeness and lawful conduct. Interest groups may supply expertise and articulate sectoral concerns, but unequal resources can give wealthy interests disproportionate access. Democratic safeguards include disclosure, open consultation and equal access to decision-makers.'''),
          AcademyLessonSection('3. Social movements and collective action', '''Social movements organise sustained collective action around shared claims, identities or grievances. Their development depends on mobilisation resources, political opportunities, leadership, networks, framing and the risks participants face. Digital platforms lower some coordination costs but do not replace organisation, trust or offline participation. Movements can widen political agendas and expose institutional failures; they can also face fragmentation, misinformation or repression. Evaluate a movement by its demands, constituency, methods, institutional response and measurable outcomes.'''),
        ], keyTerms: ['Public opinion', 'Civil society', 'Interest group', 'Social movement', 'Agenda-setting', 'Collective action'], examQuestion: 'Evaluate the role of civil society and public opinion in strengthening democratic accountability.', practiceTask: 'Map a public issue from citizen grievance to advocacy, policy response and accountability.'),
      ]),
